WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Chinchilla 2022: most models were undertrained.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:20.909
In March 2022, Jordan Hoffmann and colleagues at DeepMind revisited the question with a more careful experimental design: over 400 models from 70 million to over 16 billion parameters, trained on 5 to 500 billion tokens.

3
00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:23.941
Their conclusion contradicted the earlier reading.

4
00:00:24.221 --> 00:00:32.520
For compute-optimal training, parameters and training tokens should grow in roughly equal proportion: double the model, double the data.

5
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.637
The paper’s headline test made the point concrete.

6
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:39.693
Chinchilla, a 70-billion-parameter model trained on about 1.

7
00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:49.723
4 trillion tokens, used the same compute as DeepMind’s 280-billion-parameter Gopher but outperformed it on a wide range of evaluations.

8
00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:54.376
Many large models of the era had too many parameters for the data they saw.

9
00:00:54.656 --> 00:01:06.560
Two rules of thumb from this era are worth memorizing, because you will see them used in arguments: Training compute is about 6 times parameters times tokens (in floating-point operations).

10
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.811
A forward and backward pass costs roughly six operations per parameter per token.

11
00:01:12.091 --> 00:01:16.272
Compute-optimal training uses roughly 20 tokens per parameter.

12
00:01:16.552 --> 00:01:20.669
This is an approximation from the Chinchilla fits, not a law of nature.

13
00:01:20.949 --> 00:01:28.267
One caution that working engineers care about: “compute-optimal” means the lowest training loss for a fixed training budget.

14
00:01:28.547 --> 00:01:29.997
It ignores serving cost.

15
00:01:30.277 --> 00:01:44.720
A smaller model trained on far more than 20 tokens per parameter costs more to train for the same loss but less to run millions of times, which is why many open-weight models are deliberately “overtrained” by the Chinchilla standard.
